    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What's the difference between design-build and hiring an architect first?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Hiring an architect first means three separate contracts — designer, then bidding, then builder — and you own the gaps between them. If the drawings come back priced over your budget, you go back to the architect and pay for revisions. Design-build puts drawing and building under one contract, so the people drawing it know what it costs to build and the conflict surfaces at the drawing stage. The trade-off is that you commit to one team earlier instead of bidding a finished set to several."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does a design-build contractor actually do?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We do the front-end work a designer or architect would do — laser measuring, layout options, cabinetry and finish selections, photorealistic renderings and a permit set — and then build what was drawn, with our own crews. Where the project moves structure, a licensed New Jersey architect reviews and seals the permit drawings and structural elements are engineered and stamped. One company is accountable both for the design being buildable and for the build matching the design."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is design-build more expensive?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You pay for design either way. The difference is whether it is a separate architect's fee or a design agreement credited toward your construction. What one contract removes is the expensive middle — re-bidding when drawings land over budget, and change orders caused by drawings that did not account for what is actually behind the walls."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why do contractors charge for design?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Because it is real work with real deliverables. Measured drawings, layout studies, cabinetry planning and renderings take time from people who are paid for it. A free design is a sales tool that stops the moment you do not sign, and it is rarely detailed enough to build or price from. Ours is a design agreement, credited toward construction, and you keep the drawings either way."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you work from plans my architect already drew?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes — we price a completed set line by line and build from it, so you are buying construction from us rather than a design phase. We flag anything that will not build or will not permit before we start, in writing."
          }
        }
      ],
